 Montréal, but he shouldn’t have. The class was dominated by American " [:a {:href "/athletes/60676"} "Butch Keaser"] " who, before the final round, won one match by superior decision, and the others by falls or disqualifications. He came into the final with 0 bad points, and then defeated " [:a {:href "/athletes/57414"} "Doncho Zhekov"] " by decision, 9-5. The American coaches told Keaser, the 1973 World Champion, that all he needed to do to win the gold medal was to avoid being pinned by Pinigin, so he wrestled defensively and stayed away, losing the match 12-1, but thinking he had still won the gold medal. Unfortunately, the American coaches had it wrong, and the decisive loss gave Pinigin the gold medal, with Kaeser dropping to silver. Pinigin would go on to win World titles again in 1977-78 and placed fourth in 1980 at Moskva as a welterweight."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Round Eliminated"] [:th {} "Bad Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "331"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/60516"} "Pavel Pinigin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "348"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/60676"} "Butch Keaser"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "187"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/59097"} "Yasaburo Sugawara"]] [:td {} [:a